# expect: 10000000000
# expect: 3000000000
# expect: 2147483648
# expect: 10000000000
# expect: -294967296
# expect: true
# expect: true
# expect: 2000000001
# expect: 9
# expect: 3000000000
# expect: 0
#
# The integer model (docs/TEMEN_NUMERICS.md): an unannotated integer literal is `dyn` — an
# integer, conceptually of arbitrary precision. So literal arithmetic **promotes**; it does
# not overflow. Before jacl #112 a bare literal was `i32`, which put literal-only trees on
# the declared-width path and made `[* 100000 100000]` an overflow error.

print [* 100000 100000]
print [* 2000000 1500]
print [+ 2147483647 1]

# A wide literal and a narrow one are the same kind of thing — both dyn.
print [* 5000000000 2]

# `+% -% *%` still wrap: there the program asked for a width.
print [+% 2000000000 2000000000]

# A *declared* width still errors on overflow, and does so whichever side the literal is on.
# A literal is a leaf, so it takes the other operand's width after the fact — `[+ 1 $n]` and
# `[+ $n 1]` agree rather than differing on whether overflow promotes.
def i32 n 2000000000
print [error? [+ $n $n]]
print [error? [+ 2000000000 $n]]
print [+ 1 $n]

# A selecting form (`if`/`try`/`race`/`with-ctx`) computes nothing of its own, so an
# expectation on it is an expectation on each branch tail: this `if` is an i32 position, and
# the whole proc stays on the typed path instead of falling back to dyn.
proc pick {i32 x} i32 { + $x [if [> $x 0] { 2 } { 3 }] }
print [pick 7]

# A *constant subtree* takes its width from context, exactly like a bare literal: this
# multiply happens at 64 bits rather than promoting to dyn and needing a cast back.
def i64 wide [* 2000000 1500]
print $wide

# But adopting a width never changes the *operation*. `[/ 1 2]` is integer division whatever
# it is assigned to — the same answer C gives for `double x = 1 / 2;`. Unlike C, the crossing
# is explicit: an integer subtree adopts an *integer* width from context, and reaching an f64
# slot is a dyn -> static commitment that wants `[to …]` (TYPE_SYSTEM.md § 2).
def f64 half [to "f64" [/ 1 2]]
print $half
